WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.299 --> 00:00:09.250
In the previous part of this lesson, we’ve
seen why a convex lens is called a

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.120
CONVERGING lens!

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.779
Now let’s discuss the concave lens in detail!

00:00:14.779 --> 00:00:17.070
It refracts the light in a similar way!

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:22.900
A ray of light after entering the concave
lens bends TOWARDS the normal and when it

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.220
again enters air, it bends AWAY
from the normal.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:31.039
Will the concave lens behave in the same way
as the convex lens?

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:35.160
Will the rays
converge after refracting from a concave lens?

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.370
If the rays from a point object are incident
on a concave lens, then after

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:43.760
refraction, they do NOT converge at a single
point.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.000
These refracted rays
DIVERGE.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.609
It means they go away from each other.

00:00:48.609 --> 00:00:50.410
But wait…

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:53.690
Since they do not
intersect at a common point, does that mean

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:55.250
no image is formed?

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.100
Well the image IS formed!

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.750
If we were to extend all these rays backward,
they

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:02.809
all meet at this point.

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:06.080
The virtual image is formed at this point!

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.360
So this is a pattern by which concave lens
refracts rays of light.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:13.110
It diverges all
the light coming from a point.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.630
Hence it is called a DIVERGING lens!

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.660
Now let’s look at both the lenses side by
side!

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:24.300
So the convex lens CONVERGES all the light
rays from a point object, and the

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.530
concave lens DIVERGES all the light rays from
a point object.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:33.250
But usually our objects are big and not just
a single point!

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:36.720
So let’s use an arrow
to denote an object.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.440
We can take points on the arrow and find out
the image

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.320
for each point.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.040
Finally we join all the image points together
to get the complete

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.930
image.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.750
This is a real and inverted image!

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:51.680
This is the image formed for an object when
a

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.610
convex lens is used!

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.340
Are the object and image of the same size?

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.270
What will happen if I change the
position of the object?

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.980
Will the position of the image change?

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:05.980
Will the size of
the image change?

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:10.690
In order to know the answers to these questions,
keep watching our future

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:11.160
videos!

